CHAPTER I GENERAL PROVISIONS

Article 1 (Purpose) The purpose of this Act is to establish a foundation for research in quantum science and technology and to promote the quantum industry systematically, thereby contributing to scientific and technological innovation, national security, and the development of the national economy.

Article 2 (Definitions) The terms used in this Act are defined as follows:
  1. "Quantum science and technology" means the entirety of science and technology for implementing quantum cryptography, quantum communication, quantum sensing, quantum devices, quantum computers, and quantum simulators, and other similar technologies which involves creating systems or generating, controlling, measuring, transmitting, storing, or processing information based on quantum-mechanical properties;
  2. "Quantum enabling technology" means technology related to materials, components, equipment, systems, and software (referring to software as defined in Article 2(1) of the Software Promotion Act) necessary for the implementation of quantum science and technology;
  3. "Quantum industry" means an industry that produces products or provides services related to quantum science and technology or quantum enabling technology;
  4. "Quantum cluster" means a region designated under Article 25(1), in which enterprises, universities, and research institutes are interconnected to promote quantum science and technology and the quantum industry;
  5. "University" means the following schools and institutions:
  a. Schools under Article 2 of the Higher Education Act;
  b. The Korea Advanced Institute of Science and Technology established under the Korea Advanced Institute of Science and Technology Act, the Gwangju Institute of Science and Technology established under the Gwangju Institute of Science and Technology Act, the Daegu Gyeongbuk Institute of Science and Technology established under the Daegu Gyeongbuk Institute of Science and Technology Act, and the Ulsan National Institute of Science and Technology established under the Ulsan National Institute of Science and Technology Act;
  6. "Quantum fab" means a collective entity of personnel and facilities that support equipment and processes in order to promote quantum science and technology and the quantum industry.

Article 3 (Responsibilities of the State) (1) The State shall establish and implement comprehensive policy measures in order to promote quantum science and technology and the quantum industry.
  (2) Government-funded research institutes and national and public research institutes shall actively cooperate in the policy measures under paragraph (1).

Article 4 (Relationship to other statutes) (1) This Act shall prevail over other statutes with respect to the promotion of quantum science and technology and the quantum industry; provided, where the application of another statute is more favorable to a person receiving support under this Act, that statute shall apply.
  (2) Where quantum science and technology falls under national defense science and technology or future-leading defense technology under Article 2 of the Defense Science and Technology Innovation Promotion Act, that Act shall prevail over this Act.

CHAPTER II IMPLEMENTATION FRAMEWORK FOR PROMOTING QUANTUM SCIENCE AND TECHNOLOGY AND THE QUANTUM INDUSTRY

Article 5 (Formulation of comprehensive quantum plans) (1) The Government shall formulate a comprehensive plan for the promotion of quantum science and technology and the quantum industry (hereinafter referred to as "comprehensive quantum plan") every 5 years to systematically foster and support quantum science and technology and the quantum industry.
  (2) The comprehensive quantum plan shall include the following matters:
  1. Basic directions for promoting and supporting quantum science and technology and the quantum industry;
  2. Technology roadmaps and technology development strategies by field of quantum science and technology;
  3. Strategies to secure supply chains related to quantum enabling technology and the quantum industry;
  4. Strategies to establish and utilize quantum fabs and secure quantum fab personnel;
  5. Strategies to train specialized personnel in quantum science and technology and the quantum industry;
  6. Matters regarding the promotion of the commercialization of quantum science and technology and the fostering of enterprises related to the quantum industry;
  7. Matters regarding standardization in quantum science and technology and the quantum industry, as well as the securing and protection of intellectual property rights;
  8. Institutional improvement, international cooperation, and other related matters regarding quantum science and technology and the quantum industry;
  9. Other matters prescribed by Presidential Decree that are necessary for fostering quantum science and technology and the quantum industry.
  (3) The comprehensive quantum plan shall be formulated by the Minister of Science and ICT in consultation with the heads of relevant central administrative agencies and shall be finalized after deliberation by the Quantum Strategy Committee under Article 7.
  (4) Where the Minister of Science and ICT deems it necessary to modify the comprehensive quantum plan due to rapid changes in domestic or international conditions, the Minister of Science and ICT may amend the comprehensive quantum plan after deliberation by the Quantum Strategy Committee under Article 7; provided, where any modification concerns minor matters prescribed by Presidential Decree, deliberation by the Quantum Strategy Committee under Article 7 may be omitted.
  (5) The Government may provide administrative and financial support for the efficient implementation of the comprehensive quantum plan.
  (6) Other matters necessary for the formulation and related matters of the comprehensive quantum plan shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 6 (Formulation and implementation of implementation plans) (1) The Minister of Science and ICT shall, in consultation with the heads of relevant central administrative agencies, formulate and implement annual implementation plans (hereinafter referred to as "implementation plans") in accordance with the comprehensive quantum plan.
  (2) The Minister of Science and ICT shall report the implementation results of the preceding year's implementation plan and the implementation plan for the following year to the Quantum Strategy Committee under Article 7 on the implementation performance.
  (3) Other matters necessary for the formulation, implementation, and other related matters of implementation plans shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 7 (Establishment of Quantum Strategy Committee) (1) A Quantum Strategy Committee shall be established under the Prime Minister (hereinafter referred to as the "Quantum Strategy Committee") to deliberate and decide on the following matters regarding quantum science and technology and the quantum industry:
  1. The comprehensive quantum plan, and the master plan and development plan for quantum clusters under Articles 24 and 25 (hereafter referred to as "comprehensive quantum plan and quantum cluster-related plans" in this paragraph);
  2. The coordination of the major policies of the Government and the main functions of relevant central administrative agencies in relation to the comprehensive plan and quantum cluster-related plans;
  3. The designation of quantum clusters and the revocation or modification of the designation;
  4. Other major matters regarding quantum science and technology and the quantum industry, which are brought by the chairperson before the meeting.
  (2) The Quantum Strategy Committee shall consist of up to 20 members, including 1 chairperson and 1 vice chairperson, and the Prime Minister shall serve as chairperson and the Minister of Science and ICT shall serve as vice chairperson.
  (3) Members of the Quantum Strategy Committee shall be the following persons:
  1. The head of a relevant central administrative agency prescribed by Presidential Decree;
  2. A person commissioned by the Prime Minister from among the following persons:
  a. A person who holds the rank of associate professor or higher at a university or who has worked or is currently working at a research institute related to quantum science and technology or quantum enabling technology for at least 15 years;
  b. A person who has worked or is currently working as an executive officer or employee in quantum industry-related sector for at least 10 years;
  c. A person who is qualified as a judge, prosecutor, attorney-at-law, or patent attorney and has at least 15 years of work experience;
  d. Other persons recognized by the Prime Minister as having expertise in quantum science and technology and the quantum industry.
  (4) The Quantum Strategy Committee may establish a working committee to conduct preliminary reviews and provide practical advice on matters to be deliberated, and to deliberate on agenda items delegated to it by the Quantum Strategy Committee as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (5) An expert committee comprised of experts may be established to support the Quantum Strategy Committee and the working committee.
  (6) Matters necessary for the organization, composition, and operation of the Quantum Strategy Committee, the working committee, and the expert committee shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 8 (Fact-finding surveys) (1) In order to effectively formulate policies on quantum science and technology and the quantum industry, the Government shall conduct annual statistical surveys or fact-finding surveys on the current status of domestic and international technology, human resources, enterprises, and intellectual property rights, as well as trends in laws and systems.
  (2) Where necessary for conducting statistical surveys or fact-finding surveys under paragraph (1), the Government may request the heads of local governments; the heads of enterprises, universities, and research institutes; and the heads of related corporations and organizations to submit materials or provide opinions or other necessary cooperation. In such cases, a person requested to submit materials or provide other cooperation shall cooperate therewith unless there is a compelling reason not to do so.
  (3) The Government may entrust all or part of the tasks related to statistical surveys or fact-finding surveys under paragraph (1) to relevant specialized institutions as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (4) Matters necessary for methods, procedures, and related matters of statistical surveys or fact-finding surveys under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 9 (Establishment and operation of quantum science and technology information system) (1) The Government may establish and operate a quantum science and technology information system capable of managing the following information in order to efficiently collect, manage, and utilize information on quantum science and technology and the quantum industry:
  1. Current status of research and development in quantum science technology and quantum enabling technology;
  2. Current status of technology transfer, intellectual property rights, and standards in quantum science and technology and quantum enabling technology;
  3. Current status of workforce supply and demand in quantum science and technology and quantum enabling technology;
  4. Current status of the supply of and demand for materials and components for quantum science and technology and quantum enabling technology;
  5. Current status of enterprises in the quantum industry;
  6. Information on domestic and international facilities, including major domestic research equipment;
  7. Other information necessary for the promotion of quantum science and technology and the quantum industry.
  (2) The Government may entrust all or part of the tasks related to the establishment and operation of the quantum science and technology information system under paragraph (1) to relevant specialized institutions in accordance with Presidential Decree.
  (3) Matters necessary for the establishment and operation of the quantum science and technology information system under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 10 (Analysis of and response to impact of quantum science and technology) (1) The Government shall conduct an impact analysis on the spillover effects of quantum science and technology on affairs within its jurisdiction every 2 years.
  (2) The Government shall review security threat factors to the public and private sectors arising from the development of quantum science and technology.
  (3) The Government shall prepare response measures based on the results of the analysis and review conducted under paragraphs (1) and (2), and shall reflect them in the comprehensive quantum plan and the implementation plan; provided, this shall not apply to matters that the Director of the National Intelligence Service deems necessary to keep confidentiality for the protection of national security, advanced technologies, and industries.
  (4) Matters necessary for the method of analysis under paragraph (1), the review under paragraph (2), and other related matters shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

CHAPTER III SUPPORT FOR AND ESTABLISHMENT OF FOUNDATION FOR QUANTUM SCIENCE AND TECHNOLOGY AND THE QUANTUM INDUSTRY

Article 11 (Promotion of research and development) (1) The Government shall prepare a technology roadmap for the field of quantum science and technology as prescribed by Presidential Decree for establishing quantum science and technology development strategies and setting directions for research and development investment.
  (2) When the heads of relevant central administrative agencies initiate quantum science and technology research and development programs, they shall actively reflect the technology roadmap prepared under paragraph (1) in the identification of core technologies to be developed.

Article 12 (Special cases concerning research and development programs) (1) The head of a central administrative agency may, with respect to research and development programs related to quantum science and technology and the quantum industry that are deemed to be of high national policy importance and to involve a high level of risk associated with participation in technology development, select research and development projects and research and development institutes by means other than public competition, including designation, in accordance with the proviso, with the exception of the subparagraphs, of Article 9(4) of the National Research and Development Innovation Act.
  (2) The head of a central administrative agency may, in consultation with the Minister of Science and ICT in accordance with Article 13 of the National Research and Development Innovation Act and related provisions based on that Act, determine different government contribution support standards, cash contribution ratios, and related matters for research and development institutes participating in research and development programs related to quantum science and technology and the quantum industry; provided, in urgent cases of social or economic crisis, the head of the central administrative agency shall, without delay, after raising the support standards or lowering the cash burden ratios, notify the Minister of Science and ICT of the change and the reason therefor.

Article 13 (Grant of exclusive license) Where a patentee may, when transferring technology related to a research and development program under Article 12(1), grant an exclusive license to an enterprise or other entity receiving the technology transfer. In such cases, the patentee shall attach a condition that the grant of the exclusive license may be revoked if the enterprise or other entity receiving the technology transfer fails to use the patent at all for 3 years.

Article 14 (Promotion of commercialization) (1) In order to promote the commercialization of quantum science and technology, the Government may conduct the following projects:
  1. Demonstration and pilot projects of quantum science and technology and related products and services;
  2. Dissemination of quantum science and technology-related products and services;
  3. Support for the establishment and operation of testbeds related to quantum science and technology (referring to environments, systems or facilities for the demonstration of research and development outcomes, for standardization, as well as for the verification of reliability and safety).
  (2) The Government may designate a dedicated institution to stably and professionally conduct the projects referred to in the subparagraphs of paragraph (1), and may provide full or partial support for the necessary costs.
  (3) The Government shall evaluate the performance of the dedicated institution designated under paragraph (2) every 5 years.
  (3) The Government may revoke the designation of a dedicated institution if any of the following applies; provided, in the case of subparagraph 1, the designation shall be revoked:
  1. Where it has obtained the designation by fraud or other improper means;
  2. Where it no longer meets the requirements for designation;
  3. Where there has been no performance results for 2 years or more since the date of designation;
  4. Where the results of the performance evaluation conducted under paragraph (3) are insufficient, making it impossible to achieve the purpose of the designation.
  (5) Matters necessary for the designation requirements and procedures for dedicated institutions under paragraph (2), the evaluation of their performance under paragraph (3), and the revocation of designation of dedicated institutions under paragraph (4) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 15 (Promotion of standardization) (1) The Government may implement the following programs related to standardization in order to promote quantum science and technology and the quantum industry:
  1. Development of standards in the fields of quantum science and technology and the quantum industry and the establishment, revision, and abolition thereof; provided, with respect to matters for which standards under the Industrial Standardization Act or the Framework Act on Broadcasting Communications Development have been established, the standards shall apply;
  2. Research, dissemination, and diffusion of standards in the fields of quantum science and technology and the quantum industry;
  3. Training of personnel related to standards in the fields of quantum science and technology and the quantum industry and international exchange;
  4. Other projects prescribed by Presidential Decree as necessary for standardization in the fields of quantum science and technology and the quantum industry.
  (2) The Government may designate a specialized institution to efficiently conduct the projects referred to in paragraph (1), and may provide full or partial support for the necessary costs.
  (3) The Government may revoke the designation of a specialized institution under paragraph (2) if any of the following applies; provided, in the case of subparagraph 1, the designation shall be revoked:
  1. Where it has obtained the designated by fraud or other improper means;
  2. Where it no longer meets the requirements for designation;
  3. Other cases where it has become manifestly inappropriate for the institution to perform its functions as the specialized institution.
  (4) Matters necessary for the designation requirements and procedures for specialized institutions under paragraph (2) and the revocation of designation of specialized institutions under paragraph (3) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 16 (Startup and enterprise development) (1) The Government may implement the following programs to provide startup support for enterprises in the quantum industry and promote small and medium enterprises (referring to small and medium enterprises defined in Article 2(2) of the Framework Act on Small and Medium Enterprises; hereafter the same shall apply in this Article) and venture businesses (referring to venture businesses defined in Article 2(1) of the Special Act on the Promotion of Venture Businesses and self-employed creative enterprises defined in Article 2 of the Act on the Fostering of Self-Employed Creative Enterprises; hereafter the same shall apply in this Article) in the quantum industry: <Amended on Jan. 9, 2024>
  1. Startup support for enterprises in the quantum industry;
  2. Support for small and medium enterprises and venture businesses entering the quantum industry;
  3. Provision of office space and meeting rooms for small and medium enterprises and venture businesses in the quantum industry;
  4. Provision of information and support relating to funding, human resources, market access, and similar matters to small and medium enterprises and venture businesses in the quantum industry;
  5. Provision of consulting services for small and medium enterprises and venture businesses in the quantum industry regarding domestic and international law, accounting, management, taxation, intellectual property rights, and similar matters;
  6. Support for entry into overseas markets, including the provision of information on overseas markets related to the quantum industry;
  7. Establishment and operation of overseas expansion hubs to facilitiate the expansion of small and medium enterprises and venture businesses in the quantum industry into overseas markets;
  8. Exchange and cooperation with relevant domestic and international organizations to support small and medium enterprises and venture businesses in the quantum industry;
  9. Other projects prescribed by Presidential Decree as necessary for enterprise development and overseas expansion support.
  (2) The Government may designate a dedicated institution to efficiently implement the projects under the subparagraphs of paragraph (1) and may provide full or partial support for the necessary costs.
  (3) The Government may revoke the designation of a dedicated institution under paragraph (2) if any of the following applies; provided, in the case of subparagraph 1, the designation shall be revoked:
  1. Where it has obtained the designated by fraud or other improper means;
  2. Where it no longer meets the requirements for designation;
  3. Other cases where it has become manifestly inappropriate for the institution to perform its functions as the dedicated institution.
  (4) Matters necessary for the designation requirements and procedures for dedicated institutions under paragraph (2) and the revocation of designation of dedicated institutions under paragraph (3) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 17 (Support for establishment and operation of quantum fabs) (1) The Minister of Science and ICT may support the establishment and operation of quantum fabs in order to promote research and development in quantum science and technology and foster the quantum industry.
  (2) The Minister of Science and ICT may provide priority support to quantum fabs that meet the standards prescribed by Presidential Decree, including specialized operational personnel and facility utilization rates.
  (3) In order to provide support under paragraphs (1) and (2), the Minister of Science and ICT may evaluate the performance of quantum fabs each year and provide financial support on a differentiated basis based on the results of the evaluation.
  (4) Matters necessary for methods, procedures, and similar matters for performance evaluation under paragraph (3) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 18 (Designation of quantum science and technology research centers) (1) The Government may designate any of the following institutions or a research institute established and operated by the relevant organization as a quantum science and technology research center (hereinafter referred to as a "quantum research center") in order to support cooperation among enterprises, universities, research institutes to secure foundational technologies in the field of quantum science and technology and to link the technologies to the quantum industry:
  1. A government-funded science and technology research institute under Article 8(1) of the Act on the Establishment, Operation and Fostering of Government-Funded Science and Technology Research Institutes;
  2. The Institute for Basic Science established under Article 14 of the Special Act on Establishment of and Support for International Science and Business Belt;
  3. The Korea Advanced Institute of Science and Technology established under the Korea Advanced Institute of Science and Technology Act, the Gwangju Institute of Science and Technology established under the Gwangju Institute of Science and Technology Act, the Daegu Gyeongbuk Institute of Science and Technology established under the Daegu Gyeongbuk Institute of Science and Technology Act, and the Ulsan National Institute of Science and Technology established under the Ulsan National Institute of Science and Technology Act;
  4. A school under Article 2 of the Higher Education Act, graduate school under Article 29 of that Act, or graduate school university under Article 30 of that Act;
  5. Any other institution prescribed by Presidential Decree that is necessary to be designated as quantum research centers.
  (2) The Government shall annually review the performance of quantum research centers.
  (3) The Government may provide full or partial financial support for expenses necessary for the operation of quantum research centers. In such cases, the Government shall reflect the results of the performance review under paragraph (2).
  (4) The Government may revoke the designation of a quantum research center if any of the following subparagraphs applies; provided, in the case of subparagraph 1, the designation shall be revoked:
  1. Where it has obtained the designated by fraud or other improper means;
  2. Where it no longer meets the requirements for designation;
  3. Where the results of the performance review conducted under paragraph (2) are insufficient, making it impossible to achieve the purpose of designation.
  (5) Matters necessary for the designation requirements and designation procedures for quantum research centers, the review of their performance, and the revocation of designation of quantum research centers shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 19 (Encouragement of dissemination of quantum science and technology culture) (1) The Government may implement the following projects to enhance public understanding and knowledge level of quantum science and technology and to promote a social consensus on the importance of quantum science and technology:
  1. Awards and publicity for persons who have contributed to the promotion of quantum science and technology and the quantum industry;
  2. Publication of white papers related to quantum science and technology and the quantum industry;
  3. Support for holding lectures, seminars, events, exhibitions, etc. related to quantum science and technology and the quantum industry;
  4. Installation and operation of cultural spaces to enhance understanding of quantum science and technology and to provide opportunities for experience in the quantum industry;
  5. Other projects prescribed by Presidential Decree to disseminate the culture related to quantum science and technology and the quantum industry.
  (2) Matters necessary for methods and procedures for the implementation of projects under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 20 (Support by local governments for the quantum industry) Local governments may support enterprises, universities, research institutes, and other entities, or may contribute to or invest in relevant organizations for the promotion of the quantum industry.

CHAPTER IV HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT IN FIELDS OF QUANTUM SCIENCE AND TECHNOLOGY AND THE QUANTUM INDUSTRY

Article 21 (Support for human resource development programs) The Government may support the following programs to foster human resources in the fields of quantum science and technology and the quantum industry:
  1. Education and training for human resource development in the fields of quantum science and technology and the quantum industry;
  2. Programs to transition graduates in related fields into human resources in the fields of quantum science and technology and the quantum industry;
  3. Education related to quantum science and technology for students in elementary and secondary education courses;
  4. Support for career development of personnel in the fields of quantum science and technology and the quantum industry;
  5. Training courses designed to facilitate job placement with companies in the quantum industry;
  6. Other programs prescribed by Presidential Decree necessary for human resource development in the fields of quantum science and technology and the quantum industry.

Article 22 (Support for specialized educational institutions for quantum science and technology) (1) In order to train personnel specialized in quantum science and technology, the Government may select educational institutions specializing in quantum science and technology through public contests from among universities and graduate schools (referring to graduate schools established within universities) that have specialized personnel and facilities prescribed by Presidential Decree and provide administrative and financial support.
  (2) The Government may support universities in establishing and operating vocational education and training courses or departments, etc., in order to train personnel tailored to the demands of companies in each field.
  (3) The Government may support research institutes, non-profit corporations, organizations, etc. funded by the Government to establish and operate education centers to train professional human resources related to quantum science and technology and the quantum industry.

Article 23 (Attraction and utilization of outstanding overseas talent) The Government may implement the following projects so that domestic research entities in quantum science and technology can promote quantum science and technology and the quantum industry by attracting and utilizing outstanding overseas talent:
  1. Financial support for domestic quantum science and technology research entities that attract outstanding overseas talent;
  2. Surveys and analysis of specialized personnel in overseas enterprises, universities, and research institutes;
  3. Establishment of international networks and participation in international events for the attraction of outstanding overseas talent;
  4. Other matters prescribed by Presidential Decree necessary for attracting and utilizing outstanding overseas talent.

CHAPTER V DESIGNATION OF QUANTUM CLUSTERS

Article 24 (Formulation of master plans for quantum clusters) (1) The Minister of Science and ICT shall formulate a master plan for quantum clusters (hereinafter referred to as "quantum cluster master plan") every 5 years for the systematic formation and development of quantum clusters.
  (2) The quantum cluster master plan shall include the following matters:
  1. Basic objectives of the establishment of quantum clusters and mid- to long-term development directions;
  2. Basic directions for the designation, operation, revocation of designation, and related matters of quantum clusters;
  3. Support for capacity building and collaborative activities among enterprises, universities, and research institutes participating in quantum clusters and support for interconnection activities;
  4. Other matters deemed necessary by the Minister of Science and ICT for the establishment of quantum clusters.
  (3) Where the Minister of Science and ICT intends to formulate the quantum cluster master plan, the Minister shall hear the opinions of the Special Metropolitan City Mayor, Metropolitan City Mayors, Special Self-Governing City Mayor, Do Governors, and Special Self-Governing Province Governors (hereinafter referred to as the "Mayor/Do Governors"), consult with the heads of relevant central administrative agencies, and submit the plan to the Quantum Strategy Committee for deliberation.
  (4) Where the Minister of Science and ICT deems it necessary due to modifications in domestic and overseas trends and conditions related to quantum science and technology and the quantum industry, the Minister may modify the quantum cluster master plan.
  (5) Paragraph (3) shall apply mutatis mutandis to any modification in the quantum cluster master plan; provided, this shall not apply where any modification concerns minor matters prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (6) The Minister of Science and ICT shall annually review the implementation results and performance of the quantum cluster master plan and make the results public.
  (7) Other matters necessary for the formulation of the quantum cluster master plan, as well as for the review of performance and disclosure of results under paragraph (6), shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 25 (Formulation of development plans for quantum clusters and designation of quantum clusters) (1) Where the Minister of Science and ICT deems it necessary to develop a quantum cluster, the Minister may designate a quantum cluster after hearing the opinion of the competent Mayor/Do Governor, consulting with the heads of the relevant central administrative agencies, and formulating a quantum cluster development plan (hereinafter referred to as "quantum cluster development plan") through deliberation by the Quantum Strategy Committee.
  (2) The quantum cluster development plan shall take precedence over plans under other statutes; provided, this shall not apply to a comprehensive national land plan under Article 6(2)1 of the Framework Act on the National Land and a master plan for the management of protection zones, etc. under Article 16 of the Protection of Military Bases and Installations Act.
  (3) Where the Minister of Science and ICT designates a quantum cluster under paragraph (1), the Minister shall publicly notify the details thereof in the Official Gazette as prescribed by Presidential Decree, and notify the competent Mayor/Do Governor thereof without delay. In such cases, Article 8 of the Framework Act on the Regulation of Land Use shall apply to the public notice of topographical drawings.
  (4) Upon receipt of notice given under paragraph (3), the Mayor/Do Governor shall make the contents thereof available to the general public for inspection for at least 14 days.
  (5) Other matters necessary for the contents of and formulation procedures for quantum cluster development plans, and the designation requirements and procedures for quantum clusters shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 26 (Modification of quantum cluster development plan and revocation of designation) (1) The Minister of Science and ICT may, if necessary for the effective development of a quantum cluster, modify the quantum cluster development plan through deliberation by the quantum strategy committee and may fully or partially revoke the designation of the quantum cluster in any of the following cases:
  1. Where it is impossible or expected to be impossible to achieve the purpose of designation of the quantum cluster;
  2. Where there are grounds prescribed by Presidential Decree equivalent to those referred to in subparagraph 1.
  (2) The Mayor/Do Governor may request the Minister of Science and ICT to make a modification or revocation under paragraph (1). In such cases, the Mayor/Do Governor shall submit documents prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Science and ICT.
  (3) Article 25 shall apply mutatis mutandis to any modification in the quantum cluster development plan or the revocation of designation of a quantum cluster under paragraph (1).

Article 27 (Inspection of outcomes of quantum clusters) (1) The Mayor/Do Governor having jurisdiction over a quantum cluster shall review the operational status and performance of the quantum cluster each year and notify the results to the Minister of Science and ICT. In such cases, the Minister of Science and ICT shall make the notified results public.
  (2) Matters necessary for the review of performance and disclosure of results under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 28 (Support for quantum clusters) (1) The Government may provide administrative and financial support necessary for the establishment and operation of quantum clusters.
  (2) The Government may provide financial support on a differentiated basis based on the results of the performance review conducted under Article 27(1).

CHAPTER VI STRENGTHENING COOPERATION FOR DEVELOPMENT OF QUANTUM SCIENCE AND TECHNOLOGY AND QUANTUM INDUSTRY

Article 29 (Support for international cooperation) The Government may support the following programs in order to promote international cooperation for the development of quantum science and technology and the quantum industry:
  1. International joint research on quantum science and technology;
  2. International standardization for quantum science and technology and in the quantum industry;
  3. Overseas training and international exchange programs for domestic researchers and others related to quantum science and technology;
  4. Cooperation with international organizations and foreign governments regarding quantum science and technology and the quantum industry;
  5. International cooperation related to quantum science and technology and the quantum industry in the private sector;
  6. Hosting and participating in exhibitions and academic conferences related to quantum science and technology and the quantum industry, and attracting such events to the Republic of Korea;
  7. Other programs prescribed by Presidential Decree necessary for international cooperation.

Article 30 (Attraction of outstanding overseas research and development centers) The Government may provide support for the following matters to attract outstanding foreign research and development centers in the field of quantum science and technology determined by the Minister of Science and ICT (hereafter referred to as an "overseas quantum research center" in this Article), taking into consideration the spillover effects on the development of domestic quantum science and technology:
  1. Research and development collaboration between overseas quantum research centers operating in the Republic of Korea with domestic quantum science and technology research institutes;
  2. Personnel training and education programs conducted jointly by overseas quantum research centers operating in the Republic of Korea and domestic quantum science and technology research institutes;
  3. Support for the location of overseas quantum research centers;
  4. Other matters prescribed by Presidential Decree necessary for the attraction of overseas quantum research centers.

Article 31 (Promotion of cooperation between enterprises, universities, and research institutes related to quantum science and technology) (1) The Government may provide administrative, technical, and financial support related to the following subparagraphs to facilitate cooperation between enterprises, universities, and research institutes in the field of quantum science and technology:
  1. Joint technology development;
  2. Support for the establishment of shared infrastructure and for supply chains of materials, components, and equipment;
  3. Investment in technology transfer, research and development, production, and other related activities;
  4. Awards for outstanding enterprises; and preferential treatment in evaluations for government technology development programs and for the shared growth index under article 20-2(2)1 of the Act on the Promotion of Mutually Beneficial Cooperation between Large Enterprises and Small and Medium Enterprises;
  5. Other matters prescribed by Presidential Decree necessary to promote cooperation.
  (2) The Government may provide support to relevant associations and academic societies in order to promote information exchange, personnel exchange, collaborative research, etc. among research entities, such as enterprises, universities, and research institutes, in the field of quantum science and technology.

CHAPTER VII SUPPLEMENTARY PROVISIONS

Article 32 (Delegation and entrustment of authority) (1) The authority of the Minister of Science and ICT under this Act may be partially delegated to the heads of subordinate agencies as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (2) Part of the duties of the Minister of Science and ICT under this Act may be entrusted to relevant institutions, organizations, etc. as prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 33 (Deemed public officials for purposes of applying penalty provisions) Executive officers and employees of a dedicated institution under Article 14(2), a specialized institution under Article 15(2), a dedicated institution under Article 16(2), or a relevant institution or institution engaged in the duties delegated by the Minister of Science and ICT under Article 32 shall be deemed public officials for purposes of applying Articles 127 and 129 through 132 of the Criminal Act.

